<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened Runtime entitlements. Notarization REQUIRES the hardened runtime, and
  the hardened runtime breaks Electron without these four — so this file is not
  a loosening of security, it is the set of holes Chromium and native modules
  need in order to run under it at all.

  Each one is here for a reason, and none is a guess:

  allow-jit / allow-unsigned-executable-memory
    V8 compiles JavaScript to machine code at runtime. Without these the
    renderer crashes on launch, not gracefully.

  disable-library-validation
    sharp and @resvg/resvg-js ship prebuilt native binaries signed by their
    publishers, not by us. Library validation refuses to load a dylib signed by
    a different team, which is exactly what those are.

  allow-dyld-environment-variables
    electron-builder's own relaunch path and the Electron helpers rely on it.

  Deliberately NOT present: com.apple.security.cs.debugger, and any
  com.apple.security.device.* or network entitlement. GetVect converts images on
  the local machine; the only network it touches is the release-feed check and
  opt-in AI Enhance, neither of which needs an entitlement. If a future change
  wants one of those, it should be argued for in the diff rather than inherited
  from a template.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
